## 12. Security and Privacy Requirements

- Resumes and GitHub data can contain personal information; treat both as sensitive.
- All authentication credentials hashed/stored securely; sessions expire and are revocable.
- GitHub integration requests the minimum OAuth scopes needed (public repo read by default); private repo access requires explicit, separate consent.
- Users can disconnect GitHub at any time; disconnecting stops further syncing.
- Users can delete their uploaded resume and imported profile data; deletion cascades to derived Evidence records citing that resume.
- Private repository contents (if authorized) are used only for the stated evidence-detection purpose and are never displayed to other users.
- AI/data-processing use is disclosed to the user (what is sent to the LLM provider, and that resume/job text may be processed by a third-party AI API).
- Rate limiting on file uploads, AI-triggering endpoints, and GitHub manual sync to prevent abuse and cost overruns.

---

## 13. Edge Cases and Failure Scenarios

- Job description is too short/vague to extract meaningful requirements → prompt user to add more detail or allow manual skill tagging.
- Resume PDF is a scanned image with no extractable text → detect and inform user; offer manual entry.
- AI extraction returns malformed JSON → retry once, then fall back to manual entry with an error banner.
- GitHub account has zero public repositories → show "no evidence detected yet" state rather than blocking the flow.
- GitHub API rate limit exceeded → serve last cached sync with a "last synced X ago" indicator; queue retry.
- User revokes GitHub OAuth access from GitHub's side (not from SkillGap) → next sync attempt fails; system detects invalid token and prompts reconnect.
- User deletes a target job that has an in-progress roadmap → confirm destructive action; soft-delete with a recovery window (e.g., 30 days) before hard delete.
- Duplicate/near-duplicate skills from different sources (e.g., "JS" vs "JavaScript") → normalized via the `Skill.aliases` mapping; unmapped novel skills stored as "unmapped" pending normalization.
- User has conflicting evidence (resume claims expert-level skill, GitHub shows no related repos) → status resolves to Claimed/Unproven, not Strong, with an explanation shown to the user.
- Very large repositories causing slow scans → cap scan depth (e.g., top-level files + key config files) and time out gracefully.
- Two roadmap recalculation triggers fire concurrently (e.g., GitHub sync completes while user edits target) → recalculation is idempotent and last-write-consistent; UI shows "recalculating" state to avoid confusing the user with a half-updated view.

