## 14. Assumptions and Constraints

**Assumptions (explicitly labeled):**
- **[ASSUMPTION]** The project is built by a small student/hackathon team (1–4 people) within a limited timeframe (days to a few weeks), so MVP scope is deliberately constrained.
- **[ASSUMPTION]** GitHub evidence detection only inspects repository metadata, file presence/names, and top-level file contents (e.g., `Dockerfile`, `.github/workflows/*`, `README.md`, test directory presence) — not deep static code analysis — to keep scanning fast and inexpensive.
- **[ASSUMPTION]** "AI/LLM provider" is treated generically; the reference implementation assumes an OpenAI-compatible chat completion API with structured/JSON output support (this also covers Anthropic's Claude API, which supports structured outputs).
- **[ASSUMPTION]** Only public GitHub repository data is used by default; private repository access is an explicit opt-in not required for MVP demo purposes.
- **[ASSUMPTION]** A single default authentication method will be implemented for MVP (email/password or GitHub OAuth reused as login) rather than supporting many identity providers.
- **[ASSUMPTION]** External job-market data aggregation (§18 in the source spec) is explicitly out of MVP scope per the source material and is not built even as a stub.
- **[ASSUMPTION]** "LinkedIn/profile information" import is manual text entry only for MVP (no LinkedIn API integration, which requires special partner access).

**Constraints:**
- Must be realistically buildable and deployable by a small student team.
- Must avoid unnecessary infrastructure complexity (no microservices, no Kubernetes).
- Must keep AI usage cost-bounded (cache results, avoid redundant calls, use deterministic code where possible per source spec §27).

