## 16. Risks and Mitigations

| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| LLM hallucinates skills/evidence not actually present | High — undermines core trust proposition | Use deterministic code for GitHub/metadata detection (per source spec §27); require AI outputs to conform to a strict schema; show extracted data for user confirmation before saving as fact. |
| GitHub API rate limits throttle sync at scale | Medium | Cache aggressively, use conditional requests (ETags), queue and backoff, allow manual sync with cooldown. |
| AI provider cost overrun | Medium | Cache identical-input results, batch where possible, cap resume/job description length, rate-limit AI-triggering endpoints. |
| Users distrust a single opaque "readiness %" | Medium | Always show the score breakdown/formula (per source spec §30), never a black-box number. |
| Resume parsing produces low-quality extraction for non-standard resume formats | Medium | Always show extracted data for manual review/correction; never silently trust extraction. |
| Scope creep beyond a buildable MVP (spec lists 20+ advanced features) | High for a hackathon timeline | Strict adherence to MVP list in §9; advanced features explicitly deferred. |
| Private data exposure (resume, GitHub tokens) | High | Encrypt tokens at rest, scope minimal OAuth permissions, provide delete/disconnect controls, no cross-user data sharing. |

